      "exercise": {
        "brief": "Translate a synthetic specialty/procedure draft into a Swift value model, then demonstrate independent edits and deliberate absence handling in an isolated Swift file.",
        "steps": [
          "Declare Selection with var specialtyID: Int64? and var procedureID: Int64?, both defaulting to nil. Explain each token.",
          "Create an original containing specialty 10, assign it to edited, and change only edited to 20.",
          "Print both specialties using ??; use if let to consume a present ID and guard let in a function that requires a procedure.",
          "Predict the result of attempting to mutate a let Selection. Write the equivalent Kotlin aliasing explanation.",
          "Add one nested mutable class in a separate experiment and explain why its state can remain shared."
        ],
        "acceptance": [
          "Original retains 10 and edited contains 20.",
          "Missing procedure follows an explicit return path without force unwrapping.",
          "You distinguish a mutable binding, a value mutation, and shared reference identity.",
          "The experiment is separate from production files; attempting the work, not reading this answer, establishes completion."
        ],
        "solution": "\u003cp>Attempt the predictions first. This worked answer is a standalone Swift program with no client imports. Default values allow the omitted procedure argument; assignment creates independent outer values.\u003c/p>\u003cpre>\u003ccode class=\"language-swift\">struct Selection {\n    var specialtyID: Int64? = nil\n    var procedureID: Int64? = nil\n}\n\nlet original = Selection(specialtyID: 10)\nvar edited = original\nedited.specialtyID = 20\nprint(original.specialtyID ?? -1) // 10\nprint(edited.specialtyID ?? -1)   // 20\n\nif let specialty = edited.specialtyID {\n    print(\"Present specialty: \\(specialty)\")\n}\n\nfunc procedureText(for selection: Selection) -&gt; String {\n    guard let procedure = selection.procedureID else {\n        return \"No procedure selected\"\n    }\n    return \"Procedure \\(procedure)\"\n}\nprint(procedureText(for: edited))\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003cp>The \u003ccode>-1\u003c/code> is an output-only teaching fallback, not a stored sentinel or valid identifier. Keeping nil in the model preserves the domain fact that no procedure was selected. The local specialty binding is nonoptional only within its branch; the procedure binding remains usable after the guard.\u003c/p>\u003cp>Changing \u003ccode>original.specialtyID\u003c/code> fails because original is a let-bound struct. Kotlin assignment of a mutable data-class instance instead aliases the same object. For the nested-reference extension, reuse Notes from the lesson: assign the containing struct and mutate \u003ccode>edited.notes.text\u003c/code>. Explain the shared Notes instance explicitly rather than claiming that all struct assignments recursively clone everything. The preserved instructor example remains a reference; your own translation and explanation still need to be attempted.\u003c/p>"
      },
