      "exercise": {
        "brief": "Produce a read-only migration review of the checkout's request boundary and implement a synthetic route gateway in a scratch target. Propose one bounded change with validation and rollback.",
        "steps": [
          "Trace SearchServiceModule, the async processor, credential provider, alternate dispatcher construction, and startup selection.",
          "For each seam record isolation, lifetime, failure, account ownership, and whether the claim is directly observed.",
          "Implement a gateway that snapshots the selected implementation before awaiting a synthetic read.",
          "Use gated fakes to switch the route during an active read and verify only future reads use the new route.",
          "Write a concise review explaining fixture coverage, intentional differences, stop conditions, and unverified production rollout state."
        ],
        "acceptance": [
          "Every claim about the checkout has a verified active file anchor.",
          "The gateway preserves an in-flight request's chosen implementation.",
          "The review distinguishes route snapshotting from draining and rollback from replay.",
          "No existing client source, flag, credential, or live request is modified.",
          "The proposal has a concrete scope and caller-visible acceptance criteria."
        ],
        "solution": "\u003cp>The following standalone gateway demonstrates per-request route consistency for synthetic reads. Its actor owns selection, while both implementations satisfy one Sendable contract. Because the selected dependency is captured before awaiting it, another call to select cannot change this request's implementation midway.\u003c/p>\u003cpre>\u003ccode class=\"language-swift\">struct MigrationCard: Sendable, Equatable {\n    let id: String\n    let title: String\n}\nprotocol MigrationReading: Sendable {\n    func read(id: String) async throws -&gt; MigrationCard\n}\nenum MigrationRoute: Sendable { case existing, alternate }\n\nactor MigrationGateway {\n    private var route: MigrationRoute = .existing\n    private let existing: any MigrationReading\n    private let alternate: any MigrationReading\n\n    init(\n        existing: any MigrationReading,\n        alternate: any MigrationReading\n    ) {\n        self.existing = existing\n        self.alternate = alternate\n    }\n\n    func select(_ newRoute: MigrationRoute) {\n        route = newRoute\n    }\n\n    func read(id: String) async throws -&gt; MigrationCard {\n        try Task.checkCancellation()\n        let selected: any MigrationReading\n        switch route {\n        case .existing: selected = existing\n        case .alternate: selected = alternate\n        }\n        let result = try await selected.read(id: id)\n        try Task.checkCancellation()\n        return result\n    }\n}\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003cp>A worked review would state: “Observed: app composition creates an alternate configured URLSession dispatcher, and startup can enable it. Proposed scope: test one read operation through both injectable providers with the same synthetic fixtures. Preserve status validation, cancellation, credential adaptation, interception configuration, and tracing. Do not change account ownership or replay failed writes. Rollback selects the existing implementation for future operations. Runtime flag exposure and device performance remain unverified.”\u003c/p>\u003cp>For the route test, start a read against an existing fake and hold it, select alternate, then release the existing fake. Its result must still come from existing. Start a second read and verify alternate receives it. If draining is required, add explicit active-operation tracking and tests for when promotion happens rather than renaming this gateway.\u003c/p>\u003cp>The learning gateway does not reproduce the real client's interception, retries, draining, or tracing. It isolates one correctness property so you can reason about it. A production proposal should attach the exact operation's fixture parity results and identify the composition owner before a rollout. This is a review artifact and synthetic experiment, not a completed migration.\u003c/p>"
      },
