      "exercise": {
        "brief": "Design and inspect a synthetic 500-row scrolling screen with progress updates, stable identity, and asynchronous image placeholders. Produce a dependency and hitch investigation plan.",
        "steps": [
          "Use stable synthetic row IDs and compute any sorted/filter projection at the accepted data revision boundary.",
          "Place progress reads in a separate leaf and explain every state read in the parent and rows.",
          "Specify fixed gesture duration, Dynamic Type, device/display conditions, and cold/warm image conditions.",
          "Inspect the active search list and intrinsic-size subclass as audit evidence; retain actual trace fields as pending until execution."
        ],
        "acceptance": [
          "Inserting at the start or reordering retains the correct state and action for each domain ID.",
          "Progress updates do not trigger fresh network requests or expensive mapping from body.",
          "The report distinguishes commit and render hitches and contains no invented frame improvement.",
          "Dynamic Type, reuse image identity, route-exit cancellation, and a follow-up interaction are checked."
        ],
        "solution": "\u003cp>A worked design has a data owner that accepts a complete immutable row revision, performs the declared projection once, and exposes rows to a list. The list renders \u003ccode>ForEach(rows)\u003c/code> with stable IDs and passes each row to ResultRow. The progress leaf reads only progress. Keep the owner alive through an appropriate root state/lifetime mechanism; constructing a fresh observable owner during each body evaluation would reset both data and identity. No equality shortcut omits action or title changes.\u003c/p>\u003cp>For images, the row passes an item identity and requested pixel size to a bounded loader. An obsolete completion must verify identity before display; cancellation handles avoid wasted work after leaving the screen. Placeholders preserve sizing so image arrivals do not unexpectedly relayout the whole list. The design does not infer that a tiny frame causes downsampling.\u003c/p>\u003cp>The report labels “MainActor projection during gesture” and “render-server compositing during gesture” as competing hypotheses. Select a hitch interval, compare deadline tracks, then inspect the relevant stacks. Verify the same gesture without the heavy profiler after a candidate change. Keep separate measurements for cold image data and repeated scrolling. Expected evidence is a reproducible gesture, correctness results, trace intervals, sample count, and hitch denominator; all performance observations remain pending until the scratch app is actually run on a representative phone.\u003c/p>"
      },
