        "solution": "\u003cp>For \u003ccode>[\"b\", \"a\", \"b\", \"c\"]\u003c/code>, both functions must return \u003ccode>[\"b\", \"a\", \"c\"]\u003c/code>. Add empty, all-equal, all-distinct, and mixed Unicode fixtures using the same Swift String equality contract. Compare the complete result, not just its count. For rankedIDs, verify that equal scores use the ID tie-breaker and nonpositive scores are excluded. Do not time correctness assertions inside only one candidate.\u003c/p>\u003cp>The build sheet has independent no-op, private implementation edit, and public contract edit scenarios. Retain the same toolchain, host, destination, resolved dependencies, and declared output/cache preparation. Read timing summaries and the build timeline to identify type checking, dependency emission, linking, resources, or scripts. A source annotation change is a candidate, not a proven improvement.\u003c/p>\u003cp>The runtime sheet uses fixed datasets at several sizes, matched release configuration, observable results, and alternating baseline/candidate batches. Record raw times, sample count, median, p95 estimator, range, and allocation or peak evidence. Leave all observations pending until actual execution. The conditional conclusion is: “The membership model predicts less repeated searching with extra storage; device measurements must confirm the mapping tradeoff and a separate screen experiment must establish user impact.” This answer demonstrates contracts and reasoning without pre-completing the learner's profiling work.\u003c/p>"
