        "answerHTML": "\u003cp>The first callback unwraps its weak owner and invokes the service. Successful empty data becomes loaded with an empty payload, which the exhaustive summary renders as no matches. Offline follows the error path and renders retry. These states remain distinct even though neither displays provider names.\u003c/p>\u003cp>The saved variable strongly owns the closure, but the closure's weak capture does not own ReviewOwner. Once every other strong owner is removed, the weak probe can become nil. Calling the saved closure then reaches its absent-owner guard and returns safely. The local self created by successful unwrapping temporarily owns the instance during use. Because the initializer constructs a stored closure directly, there is no incoming plain closure parameter requiring an escaping annotation. Finally, ownership safety does not establish lifecycle correctness: an owner retained elsewhere may still be alive after its flow ends, so asynchronous extensions would need an explicit update and cancellation policy.\u003c/p>",
