        "answerHTML": "\u003cp>The root keeps its locally created observable model in \u003ccode>@State\u003c/code>, which associates storage with the root's SwiftUI identity. Recreating the View description therefore need not recreate the effective draft. An editing child uses \u003ccode>@Bindable\u003c/code> when controls require projections such as \u003ccode>$draft.name\u003c/code>. A \u003ccode>Binding\u003c/code> provides read and write access to somebody else's source of truth rather than independently storing a second draft.\u003c/p>\u003cp>A read-only child can receive a plain model property and read name or computed canContinue in its body. Observation tracks those property dependencies; it does not require that child to own the model. \u003ccode>@MainActor\u003c/code> separately defines the mutation domain. Neither annotation persists the draft, restores a process, or defines logout cleanup. Keeping identity stable preserves local state, while intentionally replacing identity can reset it. These examples require supported Observation integration and do not replace the older ObservableObject wrappers used by other lesson examples.\u003c/p>",
