        "answerHTML": "\u003cp>The timeout wins the race but does not force the other child to finish. Leaving the task-group scope waits for children, including those that were cancelled. A callback operation that ignores cancellation can therefore keep the group waiting after the nominal deadline. The timeout selection and a transport's own timeout are different mechanisms.\u003c/p>\u003cp>Define how the bridge cancels its underlying operation and how the continuation completes when cancellation races a normal callback. A checked continuation must resume exactly once; a cancellation handler may run concurrently, so protect the completion state with a suitable synchronized mechanism rather than an unsafely shared Boolean. If the source cannot be stopped, the design must state that limitation and assign an owner to any work that continues independently. Test completion-before-cancellation and cancellation-before-completion using gates, then join every task. Do not recover by swallowing cancellation into an empty success or by promising a hard deadline that the dependency cannot honor.\u003c/p>",
