        "answerHTML": "\u003cp>The struct's value semantics do not make its referenced MutableNotes safe to transfer. The let property freezes which reference is stored, not the object's mutable text. Prefer an immutable snapshot containing Sendable value fields, or keep mutable notes under an actor and access them through its isolation contract. Actors can be safely referenced across domains because their isolated members remain controlled.\u003c/p>\u003cp>\u003ccode>Sendable\u003c/code> describes transfer safety of values; \u003ccode>@Sendable\u003c/code> constrains closure captures and their use. \u003ccode>@unchecked Sendable\u003c/code> is a manual promise and adds no lock, so it needs a complete documented protection mechanism that the compiler cannot establish. Marking an accessor nonisolated opts it out of actor isolation; it cannot thereby safely read arbitrary mutable isolated state. Return a snapshot through an isolated operation instead. Likewise, referencing a MainActor model from another domain does not permit synchronous mutation of its UI properties. Diagnose the ownership and access invariant rather than suppressing the diagnostic.\u003c/p>",
