        "answerHTML": "\u003cp>The first adapter turns failure into ordinary end-of-sequence. That can be valid for best-effort notifications, but a data consumer expecting an error loses its recovery signal. Use a throwing sequence when failure belongs to the contract, or explicitly document the intended loss. Test failure separately from normal completion.\u003c/p>\u003cp>The reverse adapter starts work before subscription, so a nonreplaying subject can miss early values. It also lacks a defined stop path if subscriber cancellation never reaches the producing task. Choose subscription-driven start or deliberate replay according to the source, and assign ownership of the producer handle. For the async side, connect termination to cleanup and provide an explicit stop when an early consumer exit otherwise retains the source. Verify creation without consumption, late subscription, early stop, and completion races with controlled fixtures. These are adapter risks supported by their structure; whether any caller experiences harm still requires tracing its assumptions and observing the relevant path.\u003c/p>",
