        "androidBridge": "Android Keystore primarily exposes protected cryptographic key operations, while Apple Keychain can store password-like items and keys. Secure Enclave operations are a separate capability; Kotlin null or an exception analogy must not collapse Keychain OSStatus access failures into item absence.",
        "rubric": [
          "Separates Keychain item storage from Secure Enclave key operations.",
          "Defines service, account, class, and entitlement-controlled access group identity.",
          "Chooses accessibility from lock-state, foreground/background, and migration requirements.",
          "Distinguishes not found, access failure, duplicate/update behavior, and logout deletion scope."
        ],
        "answerHTML": "\u003cp>A Keychain session string is a protected item, not automatically a Secure Enclave key. Item identity depends on its class and attributes such as service and account, with access groups authorized through entitlements. Specify the wrapper's read, update, add, and deletion behavior so one account's cleanup does not become an overly broad deletion.\u003c/p>\u003cp>Choose accessibility from actual use. A when-unlocked, device-only policy can suit a foreground secret and prevent migration, but a background requirement may need a different policy and an explicit tradeoff. Device-only storage does not establish authorization, telemetry minimization, or every threat-model control. Preserve OSStatus categories: item-not-found differs from access, lock-state, or entitlement failure. Converting every failure into logged-out behavior can hide a signing regression or temporary access condition. Review consumers before changing a shared wrapper globally. Test policy and failure behavior with synthetic items and report actual validation separately from this design explanation.\u003c/p>",
        "followUps": [
          "What operational symptom could a missing Keychain entitlement produce?",
          "Why should a shared wrapper's accessibility change require a consumer inventory?"
        ],
        "pitfalls": [
          "Claiming every Keychain string is protected by a Secure Enclave operation.",
          "Treating every non-success OSStatus as an absent credential."
        ]
