        "answerHTML": "\u003cp>A signing identity needs the certificate and its matching private key. The profile authorizes an allowed combination of identity, application, capabilities, and distribution conditions. Requested entitlements must agree with that authorization and account setup; a certificate file alone cannot sign. Diagnose bundle mismatch, missing key, profile expiry, and entitlement problems separately.\u003c/p>\u003cp>Record the source manifest and actual build route rather than editing a generated project as the durable input. The ledger should connect commit, dependency pins, toolchain, scheme, configuration, environment, archive, version, build, test result bundles, and each binary's matching symbols. A marketing version cannot substitute for binary UUIDs. Inspect actual artifacts and signing outputs: a green simulator run does not prove distribution installation or archived resource inclusion. Simulator test hosts may still need the expected signing and Keychain context, so disabling signing universally is unsafe as a diagnostic shortcut. Keep clean-install and upgrade evidence separate when validating the candidate release.\u003c/p>",
