        "answerHTML": "\u003cp>State the problem as synthetic large-list mapping cost and define first complete value, input order, and case-sensitive identity. Assign mapper, consumer, build, and rollout owners. Compare unchanged scans with tests, set-backed membership with memory overhead, and asynchronous mapping with new lifetime and transfer responsibilities. Recommend only under explicit evidence; negligible user benefit or changed duplicate semantics can reject the candidate.\u003c/p>\u003cp>Sequence characterization fixtures, an old-path adapter, candidate equivalence, representative measurement, graph integration, controlled rollout, and removal. Require full-value comparisons and stable-ID selection checks, then accessibility, approved telemetry, and source/test inclusion in the relevant graphs. Pure shadow comparison is permitted by the absence of side effects. Define containment through the previous mapper only for installed clients that contain and honor that choice; it cannot downgrade binaries or undo unrelated data changes. Set removal criteria and an owner for migration debt. Mark device, archive, and production outcomes pending until artifacts exist instead of asking reviewers to approve an already-proven improvement.\u003c/p>",
