        "answerHTML": "\u003cp>withAnimation attaches animation information to a synchronous state mutation. The Boolean changes immediately; eligible visual differences interpolate toward that destination. It does not delay business state, make every property animatable, or keep unrelated async work alive. A transition describes a child entering or leaving the hierarchy. Applying an opacity transition to a permanently present child does not create insertion.\u003c/p>\u003cp>Keep disclosure visibility derived from the latest Boolean, so repeated taps can interrupt interpolation without leaving a contradictory final hierarchy. A value-watching animation modifier can affect other simultaneous changes inside its scope. Narrow that scope if an unrelated error or keyboard adjustment should update directly. Selection delivery needs its own ownership and exactly-once policy; waiting for a nominal duration cannot establish completion or freshness. Reduced motion should change or remove the visual interpolation while preserving the action and final state. Verify rapid toggling, interrupted navigation, and reduced-motion completion in a running target. A static screenshot demonstrates appearance at one instant, not interruption behavior.\u003c/p>",
